- [acc-intentions-111] Replace Discretionary Human Halt Authority with Pre-Programmed Enforcement Actions: Nirvana fallacy — The node implicitly contrasts imperfect, inconsistent human discretion against an idealized automated enforcement system, without fully accounting for the failure modes and gaming risks of pre-programmed triggers themselves.
- [acc-intentions-111] Replace Discretionary Human Halt Authority with Pre-Programmed Enforcement Actions: False dichotomy — The framing presents only two options — discretionary human halt authority vs. pre-programmed enforcement — excluding hybrid models that combine structured triggers with retained human override capacity for novel or unanticipated risks.
- [acc-beliefs-020] AI Feedback Dramatically Accelerates Human Learning: Hasty Generalization — Evidence of AI feedback accelerating learning in narrow, well-structured domains (e.g., coding tutorials, math problems) is extrapolated to claim 'domain mastery' broadly, without accounting for the vast range of complex, ill-defined, or tacit-knowledge-heavy fields where such acceleration is undemonstrated.
- [acc-beliefs-020] AI Feedback Dramatically Accelerates Human Learning: Equivocation — The term 'domain mastery' is used ambiguously — sliding between measurable performance benchmarks (which AI feedback can plausibly compress) and deep expert-level understanding (which is a far stronger claim), allowing favorable evidence for the former to rhetorically support the latter.
- [acc-intentions-090] Focus AI Misinformation Rules on Checkable Procedures, Not Truth Judgments: False Dichotomy — The node frames the choice as binary—either procedural verification or state truth adjudication—ignoring hybrid or contextual regulatory models that combine both without requiring governments to be sole arbiters of factual accuracy.
- [acc-intentions-075] Automate Permits, Licenses, and Compliance Checks with AI Agents: Technological Solutionism — The proposal assumes that deploying AI agents resolves administrative inefficiency without addressing the underlying policy complexity, resource constraints, or political factors that cause regulatory bottlenecks in the first place.
- [acc-beliefs-072] Courts Lack the Expertise to Weigh AI's Diffuse Benefits Against Its Visible Harms: Nirvana Fallacy — The argument implicitly compares imperfect courts against an idealized alternative institution (specialized agencies or legislatures) without rigorously accounting for the competence failures, capture risks, and democratic deficits of those alternatives.
- [acc-intentions-047] Argue That AI Requires Entirely New Laws, Not Adapted Old Ones: False Dilemma — The argument frames the choice as binary — entirely new frameworks versus unchanged old ones — ignoring that existing legal doctrines (tort, contract, administrative law) can be substantially reinterpreted or supplemented without wholesale replacement.
- [acc-intentions-047] Argue That AI Requires Entirely New Laws, Not Adapted Old Ones: Novelty (Appeal to Novelty) — The core premise assumes that because AI is new, it categorically requires new law, conflating technological novelty with legal incompatibility — a non-sequitur, as transformative technologies like electricity and the internet were largely governed under adapted existing frameworks.
- [acc-beliefs-044] Technical Challenges and Tradeoffs in Building Safe, Advanced AI: Umbrella Framing / False Unity — Grouping disparate technical claims (seed AI, monitorability tax, inference-time harm, self-correction diminishing returns) under one belief node implies they form a coherent technical worldview, when they are actually independent empirical claims with varying levels of support.

